"g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":56.007299,"lo":-3.395458,"d":1925},{"n":"Dundas Castle","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":55.975038,"lo":-3.414592,"d":1991,"wp":"en:Dundas Castle","ds":"Dundas Castle is a 15th-century castle, with substantial 19th-century additions by William Burn, near South Queensferry, on the outskirts of Edinburgh, Scotland. The home of the Dundas family since the Middle Ages, it was sold in the late 19th century and was the residence of politician and businessman Sir Jack Stewart-Clark.","wpu":"Dundas Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Dundas_Castle_South_Front_with_Porch.jpg/960px-Dundas_Castle_South_Front_with_Porch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"North Queensferry Lighthouse","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":56.008282,"lo":-3.394753,"d":2037},{"n":"Forth Bridges Light House Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":56.008307,"lo":-3.39466,"d":2040,"h":"\"by volunteer availability\"","w":"https://www.nqht.org/visit-the-light-tower-museum/"},{"n":"Deep Sea World","g":"sights","k":"aquarium","la":56.00835,"lo":-3.391136,"d":2072,"h":"Mo-Su,PH 10:00-17:00","w":"https://www.deepseaworld.com/","wp":"en:Deep Sea World","ds":"Deep Sea World is an aquarium located in the village of North Queensferry in Fife, Scotland. It is host to a collection of large sand tiger sharks, also known as ragged toothed sharks or grey nurse sharks, and various other species of shark.","wpu":"Deep Sea World","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c5/Deep_Sea_World_-_geograph.org.uk_-_701044.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Willie's Well","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":56.010062,"lo":-3.395675,"d":2232},{"n":"Staneyhill Tower","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":55.99093,"lo":-3.457584,"d":3769,"ds":"Staneyhill Tower is a 17th-century tower house in West Lothian, Scotland. The tower is located to the southeast of Hopetoun House. It is a scheduled monument of national importance being the \"remains of a 17th-century L-plan tower house which incorporates a particularly fine and unusual hexagonal tower\".","wpu":"Staneyhill Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Staneyhill_Tower%2C_Hopetoun_Estate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5701264.jpg/960px-Staneyhill_Tower%2C_Hopetoun_Estate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5701264.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Barnbougle Castle","g":"sights","k":"castle","la":55.992463,"lo":-3.334348,"d":3906,"wp":"en:Barnbougle Castle","ds":"Barnbougle Castle is a historic tower house on the southern shore of the Firth of Forth, between Cramond and Queensferry, and within the parish of Dalmeny. It lies within the Earl of Rosebery's estate, just north-west of Dalmeny House. Although its history goes back to the 13th century, the present castle is the result of rebuilding in 1881 by the 5th Earl of Rosebery, who served as Prime Minister from 1894 to 1895.","wpu":"Barnbougle Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Barnbougle02.JPG/960px-Barnbougle02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Stone Arch","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":55.955103,"lo":-3.415419,"d":4046},{"n":"Carlowrie Castle","g":"sights","k":"castle","la":55.955523,"lo":-3.375549,"d":4059,"w":"https://www.carlowriecastle.co.uk/","wp":"en:Carlowrie Castle","ds":"Carlowrie Castle was built in the Scottish Baronial style between 1852 and 1855 on the outskirts of Kirkliston, a town approximately 10 miles from Edinburgh, Scotland. It has only ever belonged to two families: the Hutchison family, who built it, and the Marshall family, who acquired it 130 years later.","wpu":"Carlowrie Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Carlowrie_Castle_4163.jpg/960px-Carlowrie_Castle_4163.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Conifox","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":55.952156,"lo":-3.394083,"d":4211,"w":"https://www.conifox.co.uk/"},{"n":"Rosyth Castle","g":"sights","k":"castle","la":56.023477,"lo":-3.431333,"d":4291,"ds":"Rosyth Castle is a fifteenth-century ruined tower house on the perimeter of Rosyth Naval Dockyard, Fife, Scotland. It originally stood on a small island in the Firth of Forth accessible only at low tide, and dates from around 1450, built as a secure residence by Sir David Stewart, who had been granted the Barony of Rosyth in 1428.","wpu":"Rosyth Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Rosyth_Castle.JPG/960px-Rosyth_Castle.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Duntarvie Castle","g":"sights","k":"castle","la":55.972648,"lo":-3.458866,"d":4305,"wp":"en:Duntarvie Castle","ds":"Duntarvie Castle is a ruined Scots Renaissance house in West Lothian, Scotland. It is located 1.5 kilometres (0.93 mi) north of Winchburgh and 9 kilometres (5.6 mi) east of Linlithgow, close to the M9 motorway. Constructed in the late 16th century, the building has been undergoing restoration since the 1990s.","wpu":"Duntarvie Castle","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Duntarvie_Castle_-_geograph.org.uk_-_684072.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Cat Stane","g":"sights","k":"archaeological_site","la":55.954824,"lo":-3.364531,"d":4402,"wp":"en:Cat Stane","ds":"The Cat Stane, or Catstane, is an inscribed standing stone near Kirkliston, on the outskirts of Edinburgh, in Scotland. It bears a fragmentary inscription dating to the fifth or sixth centuries and was part of a funerary complex consisting of the stone itself, a cairn and a series of cist burials.","wpu":"Cat Stane","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/The_Cat_Stane_at_Edinburgh_Airport_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1735737.jpg/960px-The_Cat_Stane_at_Edinburgh_Airport_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1735737.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Drum Sands","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":55.991919,"lo":-3.326102,"d":4414},{"n":"Planespotting viewpoint","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":55.952583,"lo":-3.370857,"d":4467},{"n":"Mercat Cross","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":56.03081,"lo":-3.397475,"d":4537},{"n":"Queensferry Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":55.989973,"lo":-3.398527,"d":95},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":55.989526,"lo":-3.398597,"d":112},{"n":"South Queensferry Post Office","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":55.989488,"lo":-3.398758,"d":123,"w":"https://www.postoffice.co.uk/branch-finder/2148242/south-queensferry"},{"n":"Scotmid","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":55.989363,"lo":-3.399001,"d":143,"h":"Mo-Su 07:00-22:00","w":"https://scotmid.coop/store/south-queensferry/"},{"n":"Ferryburn Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":55.985727,"lo":-3.394744,"d":495},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":55.983595,"lo":-3.39199,"d":777},{"n":"Tesco","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":55.983363,"lo":-3.401464,"d":788,"h":"Mo-Sa 06:00-24:00; Su 08:00-22:00","w":"https://www.tesco.com/store-locator/south-queensferry/5-ferrymuir"},{"n":"Port Edgar","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":55.993,"lo":-3.409,"d":817,"ds":"Port Edgar is a marina on the southern shore of the Firth of Forth, immediately west of the Forth Road Bridge and the town of South Queensferry, in Edinburgh, Scotland. Originally a naval base, HMS Lochinvar, Port Edgar is now a busy marina with a sailing school and 300 berths. The Edgar commemorated in the name is Edgar Aetheling, the brother of Queen Margaret (for whom Queensferry is named).","wpu":"Port Edgar","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/2/2e/Port_Edgar_2010.JPG/960px-Port_Edgar_2010.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Inchgarvie","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.000833,"lo":-3.386389,"d":1373,"ds":"Inchgarvie or Inch Garvie is a small, uninhabited island in the Firth of Forth. On the rocks around the island sit four caissons that make up the foundations of the Forth Bridge. Inchgarvie's fortifications pre-date the modern period.","wpu":"Inchgarvie","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/InchgarvieBridge.jpg/960px-InchgarvieBridge.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hound Point","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.0059,"lo":-3.3591,"d":2946,"ds":"Hound Point is a marine terminal off a rocky headland of that name on the southern shore of the Firth of Forth, Scotland, just east of the Forth Bridge at South Queensferry. Opened in 1975, it is owned and operated by Ineos as an oil-export terminal for North Sea oil and is the largest such facility in Scotland.","wpu":"Hound Point","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Hound_Point_marine_terminal.JPG/960px-Hound_Point_marine_terminal.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Port Laing","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.016211,"lo":-3.390092,"d":2946,"ds":"Port Laing is a small bay in North Queensferry in Fife, Scotland, and an inlet of Inverkeithing Bay within the Firth of Forth. Port Laing has a sandy beach. It lies on the Fife Coastal Path, adjacent to Carlingnose Point Nature Reserve.","wpu":"Port Laing","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Port_Laing_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7125513.jpg/960px-Port_Laing_-_geograph.org.uk_-_7125513.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Inverkeithing Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.020716,"lo":-3.379367,"d":3587,"ds":"Inverkeithing Bay is a bay on the north shore of the Firth of Forth in Scotland. Settlements on Inverkeithing Bay are North Queensferry, Inverkeithing and Dalgety Bay.","wpu":"Inverkeithing Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Scotland_-_Panorama_-_Dunfermline_-_Inverkeithing_-_panoramio.jpg/960px-Scotland_-_Panorama_-_Dunfermline_-_Inverkeithing_-_panoramio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Jamestown Viaduct","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.02297,"lo":-3.40525,"d":3701,"ds":"The Jamestown Viaduct is part of the northern approach to the Forth Bridge in Scotland. It crosses Inverkeithing, over the hamlet of Jamestown, and the village of North Queensferry in Fife.","wpu":"Jamestown Viaduct","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Jamestown_Viaduct.jpg/960px-Jamestown_Viaduct.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St Peter's Episcopal Church, Inverkeithing","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.02481,"lo":-3.40318,"d":3889,"ds":"St Peter's Episcopal Church is an Episcopal church in the town of Inverkeithing in Fife, Scotland. Built between 1903 and 1910, the church is an example of Edwardian arts and crafts architecture.","wpu":"St Peter's Episcopal Church, Inverkeithing","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9a/St_Peter%27s_Episcopal_Church_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1499911.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ballast Bank Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.028,"lo":-3.392,"d":4236,"ds":"Ballast Bank Park is a public park in the town of Inverkeithing in Fife, Scotland. Located on the banks of Inverkeithing Bay on the Firth of Forth, the park is host to the annual Inverkeithing Highland Games, as well as other community events and sports teams.","wpu":"Ballast Bank Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/Ballast_bank_park%2C_Inverkeithing.jpg/960px-Ballast_bank_park%2C_Inverkeithing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Keithing Burn","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.02916,"lo":-3.39542,"d":4355,"ds":"The Keithing Burn is a river which flows through southwest Fife in Scotland. The river falls into the inner bay of Inverkeithing Bay, in the Firth of Forth.","wpu":"Keithing Burn","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6f/Bridge_over_the_Keithing_Burn_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3356655.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Inverkeithing War Memorial","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.031945,"lo":-3.396707,"d":4664,"ds":"Inverkeithing War Memorial is a war memorial in Inverkeithing in Fife, Scotland, commemorating the 166 men of the town lost in the First and Second World Wars. The memorial is set in a memorial garden, was originally built in 1923 and is category C listed by Historic Scotland.","wpu":"Inverkeithing War Memorial","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Inverkeithing_War_Memorial_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4779176.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Spencerfield House","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":56.0346,"lo":-3.381,"d":5058,"ds":"Spencerfield House is a laird's tower house in Inverkeithing in Fife, Scotland. It was built by Henry Scott around 1592.","wpu":"Spencerfield House","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Spencerfield_House%2C_Inverkeithing.jpg/960px-Spencerfield_House%2C_Inverkeithing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Newliston","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":55.9466,"lo":-3.4251,"d":5132,"ds":"Newliston is an 18,146 sq ft (1,685.8 m2) country house near Edinburgh, Scotland. It is located 1 mile (1.6 km) south-west of Kirkliston, and 8 miles (13 km) west of the city centre. The house, designed by Robert Adam in the late 18th century, is a category A listed building.","wpu":"Newliston","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/57/Newliston_House_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1306052.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Edinburgh is the capital city of Scotland and one of its 32 council areas. It is located in southeast Scotland and is bounded to the north by the Firth of Forth and to the south by the Pentland Hills. Edinburgh had a population of 506,520 in 2020, making it the second-most-populous city in Scotland (behind Glasgow) and the seventh-most-populous in the United Kingdom.","wpu":"Edinburgh","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Skyline_of_Edinburgh.jpg/960px-Skyline_of_Edinburgh.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}